  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Here, the position of the object is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></math>, therefore the velocity of the object will be,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>d</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>d</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>d</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mo>d</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p></blockquote><blockquote><p>From the work-energy theorem, all the work done by the force acting on it will be equal to the change in its kinetic energy, i.e.,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>-</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mfenced></math><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></math><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>900</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>.</p></blockquote>",
